03, "poll_sd": 4.43, "poll_weight": 0.56, "fundamentals": {"margin": 10.42, "lean": 3.0, "environment": 7.74, "incumbency": 0.0, "money": -0.32}, "rating_margin": 7.0, "sigma_race": 4.5, "sigma_total": 5.11, "baseline_margin": 7.06, "analyst_adjustment": -0.56, "key_factors": ["Consistent polling lead for Flanagan averaging around six points, including high-quality NYT/Siena and SurveyUSA surveys", "Open seat in a state with a modest Democratic lean and a favorable Democratic environment", "Prediction markets price Flanagan near 91-92%, matching the Likely D expert consensus", "Tafoya has kept pace on fundraising, but heavy outside spending opposes Flanagan without erasing her lead"], "rationale": "Polls from mid-October through November uniformly show Flanagan ahead, with the strongest recent surveys giving her leads of five to fourteen points, and markets and most raters agree the seat stays Democratic. The polling average sits near six points, so the forecast stays there rather than at the more bullish fundamentals-only figure.", "watch": "A late polling miss toward Republican-affiliated survey numbers, which show a two-to-three point race, would move this toward Lean D.", "confidence": "high", "notes": ["Open seat: Tina Smith (D) is not on the ballot."]}, "overview": "Minnesota's open Senate seat favors Democrats, with Peggy Flanagan seeking to hold the seat Tina Smith is vacating against Republican Michele Tafoya. Every recent poll shows Flanagan leading, with margins ranging from two points in Republican-leaning surveys to fourteen in the New York Times/Siena poll, and the overall average sits near six points. Twelve expert ratings all place the race on the Democratic side, split between Lean D and Likely D, and prediction markets price Flanagan's chances around 91 percent.\n\nTafoya has raised slightly more money than Flanagan, but Flanagan benefits from far more outside support while drawing heavy opposition spending. The state's modest Democratic lean and a